---
// The front page.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE, GITHUB, DISCORD, VIDEO_ID, LOOT_VIDEO_ID, LANG_VIDEO_ID } from "../data/site";

const ld = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
  name: "Warcryer",
  applicationCategory: "CommunicationApplication",
  operatingSystem: "Windows 10, Windows 11",
  description: "Clan and alliance management for MMORPGs with voice built in: plan operations, build the roster, go live on voice that follows it, and keep attendance, points and loot. In twelve languages, with the chat translated for each reader.",
  url: SITE,
  downloadUrl: `${SITE}/download/`,
  image: `${SITE}/shots/roster.jpg`,
  offers: { "@type": "Offer", price: "0", priceCurrency: "USD" },
};
---
<Base
  title="Warcryer: run your alliance, voice built in"
  description="Clan and alliance management for MMORPGs with voice built in. Plan operations, build the roster, go live on voice that follows it, and keep attendance, DKP and loot in the same app. In twelve languages, with the chat translated for each reader. Free while in beta."
  image="roster.jpg"
  footNote="TeamSpeak, Discord, Mumble and Ventrilo are trademarks of their owners, named here only for comparison; Warcryer is not affiliated with them."
>
<script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(ld)} />

<section class="hero">
  <img class="emblem" src="/emblem.svg" alt="The Warcryer emblem: a war horn sounding" width="168" height="168">
  <h1>Warcryer</h1>
  <p class="tagline">Run your alliance. Voice built in.</p>
  <p class="lede">Clan and alliance management for MMORPGs. Plan the operation, build the roster, go live on voice that follows it, then keep the attendance, points and loot. One app, from the sign-ups to the last drop, in twelve languages, with the chat translated for each reader.</p>
  <div class="actions">
    <a class="btn" href="/download/">Download for Windows</a>
    <a class="btn quiet" href="#watch">Watch the walkthrough</a>
  </div>
  <p class="fine">Free while in beta. Windows 10 and 11. The Windows app is open source and updates itself. Not yet code-signed, so Windows warns on the first install.</p>
</section>

<section class="shot">
  <figure>
    <img src="/shots/roster.jpg" alt="The roster board of a planned castle siege: three parties with their members, class and level, the support roles each party still needs, and Reserve listing who has not been placed" width="1600" height="775" loading="lazy">
    <figcaption>The roster board of a planned siege. When it goes live, every player is put on the voice nets their place gives them.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section id="evening">
  <h2>One evening, start to finish</h2>
  <p class="intro">Most alliances run an event across a sign-up sheet, a Discord channel, a voice server and a points site. In Warcryer each step knows about the one before it.</p>
  <ol class="flow">
    <li>
      <h3>Plan</h3>
      <p>Create the operation. Every member of its clans is asked if they are coming, in the app and on Discord.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Build</h3>
      <p>Drag players into parties on the board, or place a constant party with one click.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Go live</h3>
      <p>Everyone is pulled onto their nets at once: Broadcast, Command, Clan, Party.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Call it</h3>
      <p>Orders that cut through, a pinned order, ready checks and quick calls, all over the game.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Keep it</h3>
      <p>Who was there and for how long, the points it earned, and who got the loot.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section id="watch">
  <h2>Watch an evening of it</h2>
  <p class="intro">A nineteen-minute walkthrough from the alliance leader's seat: signing in, starting an alliance and fitting it to your game, clans and invite links, the lounge, building the roster, going live, the operation chat with orders, ready checks and calls, a look at it over a real game, a briefing, a party losing its leader, the compact strip, attendance and history. It has captions and no sound. The clans and players in it are made up.</p>
  <div class="video" data-video={VIDEO_ID}>
    <button type="button" class="play" aria-label="Play the walkthrough video">
      <img src="/shots/video.jpg" alt="The walkthrough's opening card: Warcryer, a walkthrough for alliance and clan leaders" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy">
      <span class="play-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </button>
  </div>
  <p class="fine video-note">Playing it loads the video from YouTube. Or <a href={`https://youtu.be/${VIDEO_ID}`}>watch it on YouTube</a>.</p>

  <h3 class="video-more">Points and loot</h3>
  <p class="intro">Five minutes on DKP and loot: a set of points from a preset, points from an operation's attendance, the standings, your item list, a real screenshot of the drops read by the app, giving out loot, and EPGP, Zero-sum, Suicide Kings and loot councils.</p>
  <div class="video" data-video={LOOT_VIDEO_ID}>
    <button type="button" class="play" aria-label="Play the points and loot video">
      <img src="/shots/loot-video.jpg" alt="The video's opening card: Points and loot" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy">
      <span class="play-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </button>
  </div>
  <p class="fine video-note">Playing it loads the video from YouTube. Or <a href={`https://youtu.be/${LOOT_VIDEO_ID}`}>watch it on YouTube</a>.</p>
</section>

<section id="planning">
  <h2>Plan the operation</h2>
  <p class="intro">Sign-ups, characters, parties and announcements live on one board, and it knows who is actually in voice.</p>
  <div class="cards three">
    <article>
      <h3>Who is coming</h3>
      <p>When an operation is created, every member of its clans is asked in the app, and one click answers. A player bringing a different character says which, for that event only.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Mains, alts and boxes</h3>
      <p>Each player keeps the characters they play, with class and level, and marks the ones they box. Organisers see on the board what someone can bring without asking.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>What each party needs</h3>
      <p>Each party shows whether it is full and which support roles it still lacks. Click a missing role to list the unplaced players who can fill it.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Constant parties</h3>
      <p>Groups that always fight together are saved once, in order, with a leader. One click places them, and their leader can sign the whole party up.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Start from last time</h3>
      <p>A new operation can begin with the parties, leaders and deputies of an earlier one. Players who have left or been banned since are left out.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Told on Discord</h3>
      <p>Connect a channel and every new, changed, started or cancelled operation is announced there, in each reader's own time zone, with a reminder before the start.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="voice">
  <h2>Voice that follows the roster</h2>
  <p class="intro">Nobody picks a channel. Your place on the roster decides which nets you hear and which you may speak on, each with its own push-to-talk key, and moving a player moves their voice.</p>

  <ol class="nets">
    <li style="--net:#e2553f">
      <h3>Broadcast</h3>
      <p class="who">Event leader and deputies speak</p>
      <p>The call everyone hears, for the orders that move the whole alliance.</p>
    </li>
    <li style="--net:#e6c77a">
      <h3>Command</h3>
      <p class="who">Leaders only</p>
      <p>Event, clan and party leaders plan here without the rank and file hearing it.</p>
    </li>
    <li style="--net:#8c86ff">
      <h3>Clan</h3>
      <p class="who">Clan leader and officers speak</p>
      <p>Each clan's own orders, heard by that clan alone.</p>
    </li>
    <li style="--net:#5fd35f">
      <h3>Party</h3>
      <p class="who">Everyone in the party speaks</p>
      <p>Where the fighting is called. Push-to-talk or voice activation.</p>
    </li>
    <li style="--net:#9a968c">
      <h3>Reserve</h3>
      <p class="who">Players waiting for a party</p>
      <p>Latecomers wait here with the organisers until they are placed.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h3>A leader's voice cuts through</h3>
      <p>While someone speaks on a higher net, the nets below it drop to a murmur and come back when the call ends. Nothing is muted, and the lowered nets say so on screen.</p>
      <h3>Keys that work in the game</h3>
      <p>Push-to-talk keys, mouse side buttons included, work while the game has focus. They are never taken from the game, and no administrator rights are needed.</p>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <img src="/shots/lowered.jpg" alt="Two players side by side: the leader holds Broadcast while the member's party net is marked as lowered" width="1600" height="775" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>Left, the leader holds the Broadcast key. Right, the member's party net is lowered under the order.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="split flip">
    <figure>
      <img src="/shots/handover.jpg" alt="Two players side by side, each with a bar across the top: Orin has dropped out, Vanguard has no one on Command, who leads it now, with buttons to choose" width="1600" height="775" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>The party's leader has dropped out. The party and the clan's leader are asked who leads now.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div>
      <h3>A party is never cut off</h3>
      <p>If a party leader's game crashes, the party and the clan's officers are asked after about thirty seconds who leads now. One click settles it.</p>
      <h3>And if nobody answers</h3>
      <p>Thirty seconds later the next member in party order is promoted, told, and on Command at once.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="chat">
  <h2>Orders that stay on screen</h2>
  <p class="intro">Every room has a text chat: an operation has a channel for each net you are on, and the lounges and groups have theirs. What was said by voice can be pinned, checked and answered without anyone typing in the middle of a fight.</p>
  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h3>A pinned order</h3>
      <p>The event leader pins an order on Broadcast. It stays at the top of everyone's chat and on their strip until it changes.</p>
      <h3>Ready checks</h3>
      <p>One click asks everyone; the answers count up as they come in: ready, not ready, no answer.</p>
      <h3>Quick calls</h3>
      <p>Need res, Need heal, Out of mana: a short line sent with one click or one key. The alliance sets its own calls.</p>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <img src="/shots/chat.jpg" alt="Two players side by side: the leader's chat with a pinned order and a ready check counting answers, and a member's party chat with quick calls" width="1600" height="775" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>Left, the leader's chat: a pinned order and a ready check. Right, a member's party chat with quick calls.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="split flip">
    <figure>
      <img src="/shots/ingame.jpg" alt="A real game with the Warcryer chat window in the lower left corner, showing the operation's channels, a ready check button and quick calls" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>The chat window over a real game. Names are blurred.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div>
      <h3>Over the game, not in it</h3>
      <p>The compact strip shows who is talking on which net, and the chat can open in the same small window. It stays on top of the game in windowed or borderless mode.</p>
      <h3>Nothing read, nothing injected</h3>
      <p>It is an ordinary window you drag where you like. Nothing is drawn into the game and nothing is read from it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="languages">
  <h2>One alliance, many languages</h2>
  <p class="intro">An alliance is clans from different countries sharing one chat. In Warcryer each player reads that chat, and the app itself, in their own language.</p>
  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h3>The chat, in each reader's language</h3>
      <p>A line appears at once, as it was written. About a second later it is in your language and marked as translated. Click the mark to see the original.</p>
      <h3>Game talk stays game talk</h3>
      <p>Buff, res, class abbreviations and names are kept the way players write them. An alliance can add its own slang and the names that must be left alone.</p>
      <h3>Each reader decides</h3>
      <p>Translation is your own switch. What you write is never changed for you, and a clan mate with it off reads your line exactly as you typed it.</p>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <img src="/shots/languages.jpg" alt="Two chat windows over a game, showing the same five lines: on the left every line is in English, on the right in Russian, and each line that was translated carries a small mark" width="1600" height="775" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>The same chat in two players' windows over the game. Left, a player who reads English. Right, one who reads Russian.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  {LANG_VIDEO_ID && (
    <>
      <h3 class="video-more">Watch it: two minutes</h3>
      <p class="intro">One clan whose players write in Russian, Polish, Greek and Portuguese, seen from the seat of a player who reads English and one who reads Russian, over a real game. The translations are the ones the app made while it was recorded.</p>
      <div class="video" data-video={LANG_VIDEO_ID}>
        <button type="button" class="play" aria-label="Play the video Chat in your language">
          <img src="/shots/languages-video.jpg" alt="The video's opening card: Chat in your language" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy">
          <span class="play-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <p class="fine video-note">Playing it loads the video from YouTube. Or <a href={`https://youtu.be/${LANG_VIDEO_ID}`}>watch it on YouTube</a>.</p>
    </>
  )}
  <div class="cards three">
    <article>
      <h3>The app in twelve languages</h3>
      <p>English, French, German, Spanish, Brazilian Portuguese, Romanian, Polish, Lithuanian, Greek, Russian, Ukrainian and Turkish. Every tab, the notices and the compact strip, chosen by each player for themselves.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Orders and calls too</h3>
      <p>A pinned order and a quick call are translated like any other line, and a ready check asks each player in their own language, in the app and in the chat window over the game.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Honest about the machine</h3>
      <p>The translating is done by Claude, a language model, and it is sometimes wrong. The original is always one click away, and an alliance's admins can switch translation off for everyone.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="loot">
  <h2>Points and loot, from the same evening</h2>
  <p class="intro">Points come from who was really in voice, loot goes to the right player, and everyone can check the standings. No second site to keep in step.</p>
  <div class="cards three">
    <article>
      <h3>Your way of sharing</h3>
      <p>Start from a preset: attendance only, classic DKP, DKP by the hour, EPGP, Zero-sum, Suicide Kings, fleet participation, or your own rules. A loot council needs no points at all.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Earned from attendance</h3>
      <p>When an operation ends, the app drafts everyone's points from their time in voice, with the reason beside each amount. Change anything, then post.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Standings everyone can read</h3>
      <p>Balances highest first, and every line that made them. A change by hand is a line of its own with its reason, so nothing is quietly edited.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Drops read from a screenshot</h3>
      <p>Give the app a screenshot of the drops in the game's chat. It lists each item and how many, spelled as on your item list, for you to check and add.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Give it out</h3>
      <p>Who got it, which character, which points pay, and the price from your list. A mistake is put right with Take back, and the log says so.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Decay and seasons</h3>
      <p>Take a share off every balance each week or month so old points do not rule for ever, and start a new season with part carried over or none.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <figure class="wide">
      <img src="/shots/loot-read.jpg" alt="An operation's loot panel reading a real screenshot of a raid boss's drops from the game's chat, shown beside it" width="1600" height="775" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>A real screenshot of a Queen Ant kill, given to the app on the operation's loot panel.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="wide">
      <img src="/shots/points-draft.jpg" alt="The points draft after an operation: each player's time in voice, whether they said they were coming, the reason for their points and the amount" width="1600" height="775" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>The points draft after an operation: time in voice, the reason, and the points.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section id="kept">
  <h2>Everything is kept</h2>
  <p class="intro">Every operation keeps its roster, its attendance, its points and its loot, for as long as the alliance wants them.</p>
  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h3>Attendance</h3>
      <p>Who was in voice and for how long, counted from the start. Late arrivals, early leavers and players who said they were coming and never showed are plain to see.</p>
      <h3>History</h3>
      <p>Turnout from one operation to the next, and every player's record: how often they come, how long they stay, when they were last there.</p>
      <h3>The log</h3>
      <p>Every approval, move, role change, ban, points line and drop, with who did it, in plain sentences. Admins see everything, clan leaders their clan, each player their own.</p>
    </div>
    <figure>
      <img src="/shots/history.jpg" alt="The History tab: six operations in three months, average turnout of 65 percent, and a turnout chart with one column per operation" width="1600" height="775" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>The History tab: turnout per operation over three months.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="between">
  <h2>Between operations</h2>
  <p class="intro">Most evenings are not a siege. The same app is where the alliance sits the rest of the time, and one screen shows it: your nets on the left, your room in the middle, your alliance as it is right now on the right.</p>
  <div class="cards three">
    <article>
      <h3>Groups</h3>
      <p>Any member starts a small room for a dungeon, a farming spot or a chat, and anyone in the alliance can join it, whatever their clan. The clan's lounge stays audible underneath, quieter.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Who is online</h3>
      <p>Every group with the players in it, and everyone else who has the app open: in the lounge, in an operation, or away.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Chat in every room</h3>
      <p>The lounges and groups have a chat of their own, with quick calls, and it can sit over the game like the operation's.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Leaders are still heard</h3>
      <p>A clan's leader and officers speak to their clan between events as they do during one, and an alliance can give its admins a net that reaches every clan.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Call to arms</h3>
      <p>A world boss is up, or a castle is under attack. An admin starts an operation that is live at once: every group becomes a party and everyone is on its nets.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>One line for what waits</h3>
      <p>An event asking if you are coming, a player asking to join your party, a new version: one line under the tabs, the most pressing first.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <figure class="wide">
    <img src="/shots/between.jpg" alt="The Voice tab between operations: the player's nets on the left, the clan's lounge with its chat in the middle, and on the right the alliance's groups and who is online" width="1600" height="775" loading="lazy">
    <figcaption>An ordinary evening: your nets, your room and its chat, and the alliance's groups and who is online.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section id="leaders">
  <h2>Run the alliance from the same window</h2>
  <p class="intro">Each screen offers only what your role allows, and the server checks every action again.</p>
  <div class="cards">
    <article>
      <h3>Clans and invite links</h3>
      <p>Players come in by an invite link from their clan's leader, with a set number of uses and days, straight in or on a waiting list.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Roles that mean something</h3>
      <p>Owner, admins, clan leaders and officers, event leaders and deputies, party leaders. Voice rights change with the role.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>The lounge</h3>
      <p>Each clan has a lounge to sit and talk in, and the alliance can open one shared by every clan. When an operation ends, everyone is returned there.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Briefings</h3>
      <p>The event leader can lock Broadcast open and talk without holding a key. It releases itself if forgotten.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="alliance">
  <h2>Your alliance, your game, your words</h2>
  <p class="intro">Nothing about any one game is built in. An alliance sets the app up for the game it plays, and what is inside an alliance is seen and heard by nobody outside it.</p>
  <div class="cards">
    <article>
      <h3>An alliance of your own</h3>
      <p>Start one from the app with a name and a game. You own it: create its clans, appoint admins, and hand it on if you step down. Nobody can find it or ask to join from outside.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Fits your game</h3>
      <p>Classes and roles, party size, level or gear bands, your own kinds of event. Start from the lists for Lineage 2, World of Warcraft or Throne and Liberty, or from nothing.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>In your own words</h3>
      <p>If your players say guild where the app says clan, or raid where it says operation, say so once. The app, its guides and the Discord announcements then speak your way.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Kept apart</h3>
      <p>Every release is tested by having one alliance's players try everything against another's, and all of it must be refused.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <figure class="wide">
    <img src="/shots/setup.jpg" alt="The game setup editor: an alliance's own kinds of event, group types and level bands, and the words the app is built around, each with a box for the alliance's own word" width="1600" height="775" loading="lazy">
    <figcaption>Game setup: your own kinds of event, and your own words for what the app is built around.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section id="open">
  <h2>Open source, and quiet about your data</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <article>
      <h3>The client is public</h3>
      <p>The Windows app is MIT licensed. Every release is built from the public repository and installed and tested on a clean Windows machine before it is published.</p>
      <p><a href={GITHUB}>Read the source</a></p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Sign in with Discord</h3>
      <p>Sign-in happens in your own browser. The app asks Discord only who you are: no email address, no server list, no messages.</p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Nothing recorded</h3>
      <p>Voice is relayed live and not stored. Key presses are not logged or sent. No telemetry, no ads. A drop screenshot a leader asks to have read is sent to Anthropic for reading and not kept. You can delete your account from the app.</p>
      <p><a href="/privacy/">Read the privacy policy</a></p>
    </article>
    <article>
      <h3>Signed updates</h3>
      <p>The app keeps itself up to date and accepts an update only if it carries the project's signature. Nothing installs mid-event.</p>
      <p><a href="/guides/members/#updates-and-the-tray-icon">How updates work</a></p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="compare">
  <h2>Coming from another voice app?</h2>
  <p class="intro">TeamSpeak, Discord, Mumble and Ventrilo give you channels and leave the rest to you. See what changes, and where each of them is still the better choice.</p>
  <div class="actions" style="justify-content:flex-start">
    <a class="btn quiet" href="/compare/teamspeak/">vs TeamSpeak</a>
    <a class="btn quiet" href="/compare/discord/">vs Discord</a>
    <a class="btn quiet" href="/compare/mumble/">vs Mumble</a>
    <a class="btn quiet" href="/compare/ventrilo/">vs Ventrilo</a>
    <a class="btn quiet" href="/compare/">All side by side</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="status" class="status">
  <h2>Free while in beta</h2>
  <p>Warcryer is in beta and nothing is charged for it. An alliance has room for 300 approved members. It is still being tested: expect rough edges, and tell us about them.</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Download</strong> the Windows app and sign in with Discord.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask for a beta code</strong> on the Warcryer Discord. One code starts one alliance; the players who join it need none.</li>
    <li><strong>Start your alliance</strong> in the app and follow <a href="/guides/first-hour/">Your alliance's first hour</a>: clans, invite links for their leaders, and a first planned operation.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="actions">
    <a class="btn" href="/download/">Download for Windows</a>
    <a class="btn quiet" href={DISCORD}>Join the Discord</a>
    <a class="btn quiet" href="/guides/">Read the guides</a>
  </div>
</section>

</Base>

<script>
  // A video is fetched from YouTube only when someone asks to play it.
  for (const box of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-video]")) {
    box.querySelector(".play")?.addEventListener("click", () => {
      const frame = document.createElement("iframe");
      frame.src = `https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/${box.dataset.video}?autoplay=1&rel=0`;
      frame.title = box.querySelector(".play")?.getAttribute("aria-label")?.replace(/^Play the /, "") ?? "Warcryer video";
      frame.allow = "autoplay; encrypted-media; picture-in-picture; fullscreen";
      frame.allowFullscreen = true;
      box.replaceChildren(frame);
    });
  }
</script>
